## cy.log vs cy.task

```js
cy.log('now on step 3'); // Command Log only

// cypress.config.ts
setupNodeEvents(on) {
  on('task', {
    log(message) {
      console.log(message);
      return null; // tasks must return null or a value
    },
  });
}

cy.task('log', { spec: 'leave', violations: 2 });
```

In `cypress run` on GitHub, `cy.log` may never show in the job log. `cy.task('log')` prints from Node — that is the CI-visible channel. Same pattern for seeding the DB or reading a file you cannot see from the browser.

## Error handling you should actually configure

```js
Cypress.on('uncaught:exception', (err) => {
  if (err.message.includes('ResizeObserver loop')) return false;
  return true; // fail the test
});
```

A blanket `return false` means Bizlevate production exceptions will not fail CI. Name the exception.

`cy.visit('/missing', { failOnStatusCode: false })` is for asserting an error page. Do not set it globally.

`cy.on('fail', ...)` can screenshot extra context; do not turn fails into passes.

## Versus Playwright and Selenium

Playwright tests already run in Node — `console.log` shows in CI; `test.info().attach` puts files on the report. That is closer to **`cy.task`** than to `cy.log`. Selenium + TestNG/JUnit logs in the JVM. The Cypress-specific skill is remembering the **browser spec vs Node tasks** split.

Interview line: "`cy.log` is the GUI. `cy.task` logs in Node for CI. I do not globally swallow `uncaught:exception`. Playwright doesn't need this split because the test already lives in Node."
